{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790333676,"nonce":"doyMA83GTirsckpw+M/dL8xKfdg=","message":"Cause found and fixed in 0f225f2805c44dc0013fc029307bbdd1a3ae5bd2: a calling-convention mismatch that only Windows x64 can see.\n\nSSA lowering gave every lambda HirFunction CallingConvention::Fast, and the Cranelift backend compiled Fast as CallConv::Fast. Every call that reaches a lambda goes through a pointer: HirCallable::Indirect, IndirectCall, a call cell, or the interpreter's thunk for an address. All of those build their signature with module.make_signature(), which is the platform convention. On aarch64 and on x86_64 SysV, Cranelift's Fast passes register arguments the way the platform does, so this worked there. On Windows x64, Fast uses the SysV registers (rdi, rsi, ... with rdi/rsi caller-saved), while WindowsFastcall passes rcx, rdx, r8, r9 and treats rsi/rdi as callee-saved.\n\nEvidence (the kernel's HIR compiled with CraneliftBackend::for_target(\"x86_64-pc-windows-msvc\") on the NUC, at main plus the new check):\n- CLIF of main: `sig1 = (i64, i64) -\u003e i64 fast` for the lambda's func_addr, `sig4 = (i64, i64) -\u003e i64 windows_fastcall` on `v27 = call_indirect.i64 sig4, v14(v0, v1)`.\n- The check: \"inst39 in `u0:83` calls `__lambda_HirId(659)__HirId(659)` with the windows_fastcall convention, but `__lambda_HirId(659)__HirId(659)` is compiled with fast\". On the host (x86_64 Linux) the same check reported system_v against fast.\n- The machine code in the classic runtime's configuration (OSR probes off): main keeps the loop counter i in %rsi (callee-saved, saved in the prologue) and passes the arguments in rcx/rdx. The lambda reads acc from %rdi and i from %rsi, then computes `subq %r8, %rsi`, which leaves i % 8 in %rsi. Back in main, i becomes i % 8 + 1 on every iteration and never reaches 10,000,000, so the loop never ends. That is the hang the watchdog reported.\n\nFix: every function Cranelift compiles uses the ISA default convention whatever its HIR says (translate_signature no longer maps Fast/WebKit to CallConv::Fast), NativeSig no longer carries a `fast` flag (the interpreter's thunks always use the platform convention), and lambdas are lowered as C. crate::abi documents the rule. LLVM already declared every internal function with the C convention (its dlsym set holds them all), so it did not change.\n\nGuard: debug builds panic on any call_indirect whose target resolves (func_addr, call-cell load, select, block parameter) to a function declared with another convention. crates/compiler/tests/call_convention_agreement.rs (closure, reference, and both through call cells) failed 4 of 4 at main on the NUC and passes after the fix. crates/zynml/tests/a_lambda_is_called_with_its_convention.rs runs the kernel on the host and compiles it for Windows x64 (x86_64 hosts only, since the Mac build has no x86 ISA). Both failed at main and pass after the fix.\n\nVerified on the Mac (aarch64, debug) and the NUC (x86_64, debug): zyntax_compiler, zynml (bench_memory_probe included) and zyntax_embed all pass, Lua conformance 36/36, Python conformance 50/50, and fmt. Workspace clippy, run as CI runs it, is clean on the NUC. The 37 Lua/Python bench kernels give the same output as lua5.4/python3 on release llvm-backend builds on both machines. No Windows machine was available, so the next Windows CI run is the real confirmation. If bench_memory_probe still hangs, reopen this issue.","files":null}]}